 "outcomeNote": "Rava's diyuk supports Rav Pappa's limitation",
 "continuesQuestionOf": null,
 "segments": [
  {
   "id": "avodah_zarah.20b.4",
   "daf": "20b",
   "sentenceId": 3,
   "he": "וְלֹא בְּבִגְדֵי צֶבַע [שֶׁל] אִשָּׁה. אָמַר רַב יְהוּדָה אָמַר שְׁמוּאֵל: אֲפִילּוּ שְׁטוּחִין עַל גַּבֵּי כּוֹתֶל. אָמַר רַב פָּפָּא: וּבְמַכִּיר בַּעֲלֵיהֶן. אָמַר רָבָא: דַּיְקָא נָמֵי, דְּקָתָנֵי ״וְלֹא בְּבִגְדֵי צֶבַע אִשָּׁה״, וְלָא קָתָנֵי ״וְלֹא בְּבִגְדֵי צִבְעוֹנִין״, שְׁמַע מִינַּהּ.",
   "en": "With regard to the statement in the baraita: «Nor» may one gaze «at the colored garments of a woman, Rav Yehuda says» that «Shmuel says:» This is the halakha «even» if «they are spread on a wall,» not only when they are being worn. «Rav Pappa says: And» the prohibition applies only «when one knows their owner. Rava said:» The language of the baraita «is also precise, as it teaches: Nor» may one gaze «at the colored garments of a woman, and it does not teach: Nor» may one gaze «at colored garments. Learn from it» that the prohibition applies only to the garments of one he knows."
  }
 ],
